## 📋 Overview

### Feature Description
Setup the foundational architecture for the "School Match Platform". This includes establishing the Next.js + NestJS monorepo, designing the initial PostgreSQL database schema with Prisma, implementing specialized JWT authentication for different user types (Schools vs Teachers/Businesses), and creating the basic mobile-responsive UI layout.

### Success Criteria
- [x] Monorepo established with Next.js (Frontend) and NestJS (Backend).
- [x] PostgreSQL database running (Docker) and connected via Prisma.
- [x] User table created with distinction for `ROLE` (School, Teacher, Business).
- [x] Authentication endpoints (Sign-up, Login, Refresh) working with JWT.
- [x] Frontend successfully authenticates and stores tokens.
- [x] Basic responsive layout (Mobile First) implemented.

### User Impact
- **Schools/Teachers**: Will be able to sign up and log in securely.
- **Developers**: Will have a stable, type-safe environment to build future features.

---

## 🏗️ Architecture Decisions

| Decision | Rationale | Trade-offs |
|----------|-----------|------------|
| **Monorepo** | Unified configs, shared types between FE/BE. | Initial setup complexity. |
| **NestJS (Backend)** | Structured, scalable, TypeScript-first. | Steeper learning curve than Express. |
| **Next.js (Frontend)** | SEO-friendly (SSR), powerful routing. | Vercel dependency for easiest deploy. |
| **PostgreSQL** | Reliable for complex relational data (users, schools, jobs). | Slightly more config than NoSQL. |
| **Prisma** | Type-safe DB access, auto-migrations. | Abstraction layer overhead. |
| **JWT Auth** | Stateless, scalable authentication. | Token management (refresh tokens) needed. |

---

